  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 236 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nWednesday through Sunday:\n\nSurface high pressure will settle over the northeast U.S. while\nthe west half of the country will remain in zonal flow\nWednesday. A weak mid level shortwave trough trekking along the\nnorthern Plains will bring an area of warm air advection and\nvorticity advection into southern WI Wednesday afternoon and\nnight. The associated surface trough may take until Thursday\nafternoon to clear southeast WI since the high out east is so\nstrong. \n\nThe mid level moisture is going to be limited with this system\nso the forecast is for light rain showers that do not amount to\nmuch precip (less than a quarter inch). We could have a little \nelevated instability as the low level jet pushes into the region\nwith the warm air advection Wed nt, so a few lightning strikes \nwill be possible. \n\nThe forecast is trending drier for Thursday night/Friday as\nsurface high pressure moves back over the Upper Great Lakes.\nThis high used to have a colder core over us, but that will now\nbe shunted well to the north for the weekend. Our next \nreasonable chance for rain showers will be Saturday afternoon\nand night, but these will be light once again in a weak warm air\nadvection regime. However, the source region of the moisture \nwill have a Pacific origin this time, so steadier showers could \nbe possible. It is still a little too early to say for sure. We \nwill keep an eye on this one since it would happen on an evening\nwhere many people are outside. \n\nAny lingering rain will exit by Sunday afternoon-evening as high\npressure pushes back in. \n\nCronce",
